@@ -0,0 +1,161 @@
+# Resident policy extension
+
+Thin downstream integration for Hindsight **v1.2.1**, upstream commit
+`0eb02d8c418eac6b489bc63883c993b2257dfac4`. It adds authenticated principal-to-bank
+permissions through the existing Tenant and OperationValidator interfaces. It does
+not modify the engine, add a second memory store, or expose a new API. The upstream
+MIT license applies; preserve the root `LICENSE` when distributing this source.
+
+The agent talks to an AgentOps facade. That facade derives the bank, source revision,
+tags, and authority from its own verified run context. The agent never receives
+this extension's bearer credentials or chooses its own security scope. Keep the
+API, PostgreSQL, gateway, configuration and credentials outside the model-writable
+workspace. A bearer principal is the facade/service, not a claim supplied by a model.
+
+## Policy contract
+
+Start from `policy.example.json`; the example hashes are unusable placeholders.
+Generate high-entropy service credentials with the host's credential tooling and
+store only their lowercase SHA-256 hashes in the policy. The policy is a protected,
+absolute JSON path, loaded once on process startup. Both API and worker load the
+same version. Unknown settings, unknown operations, duplicate banks, missing scope
+fields and empty tag lists fail startup. Never reload it from an agent worktree.
+
+* Each principal has exact bank IDs and an explicit operation list. No bank or
+  operation wildcards. `read_tags` requires **all** listed tags with upstream
+  `all_strict` matching; caller filters can only narrow the result. `write_tags`
+  uses upstream shell-pattern checks, and every retained item/fact must also carry
+  all required read tags. `null` explicitly grants unrestricted bank scope;
+  omission is an error. For a read-only grant, omit write operations.
+* The operation names are upstream `BankReadOperation` / `BankWriteOperation`
+  values plus the separately dispatched `retain`, `recall`, `reflect`,
+  `consolidate`, `create_bank`, `get_mental_model`, `refresh_mental_model`, and
+  `list_banks` hooks. Mental-model prechecks map to `create_mental_model` /
+  `refresh_mental_model`. The exact closed set is `policy.OPERATIONS`.
+* Tenant authentication stamps a non-secret key ID into queued operations. Its ID
+  changes with the token hash. User-initiated async retain rechecks that identity,
+  bank, operation, item tags and extracted-fact tags in the worker. A removed or
+  rotated key cannot authorize a queued retain after the worker restarts.
+  Upstream internal execution does not re-run every existing-document read-by-ID
+  check if another writer retags that document after enqueue. The facade must use
+  immutable, scope-bound source-revision document IDs and control every writer;
+  this is not a shared untrusted-tenant write service.
+* `internal_grants` is a separate exact bank/operation allowlist for maintenance
+  operations reaching validator hooks. Empty denies those operations. Upstream
+  housekeeping outside these hooks still follows the service settings.
+  Maintenance carrying an originating key also
+  requires that key still exists and still has the bank. The default example
+  grants consolidation plus its nested recall and bank-profile read. User-initiated
+  work cannot use these grants.
+* Hindsight intentionally bypasses tag resolvers for internal work. Consolidation
+  is therefore explicitly **bank-wide**. Use a bank per project/trust boundary;
+  do not mix mutually untrusted projects and treat tag filters as an LLM sandbox.
+  This extension also does not implement source freshness or authority: the
+  facade must resolve/recheck sources and fence stale results before injection.
+
+Bank configuration mutation is denied by the tenant extension as well as disabled
+in service configuration. Scoped callers are additionally refused whole-bank
+operations by the upstream engine. Hooks are a version-pinned defense, not a
+guarantee that every future API route automatically acquires authorization. Keep
+the raw API private and expose a narrow facade allowlist; requalify endpoint and
+method coverage before an upstream upgrade.
+
+## Build and run
+
+Build from this repository root. The first image uses the pinned source checkout;
+capture the resulting image digest and use it for the deployed extension image.
+Upstream's Dockerfile resolves OS packages during build, so the source commit alone
+is not a reproducible OS image. Record the image digest/SBOM as part of the release.
+
+```sh
+docker build -f docker/standalone/Dockerfile --target api-only \
+  --build-arg INCLUDE_LOCAL_MODELS=false --build-arg PRELOAD_ML_MODELS=false \
+  -t resident-hindsight-base:1.2.1 .
+docker build -f hindsight-extensions/resident/Dockerfile \
+  --build-arg HINDSIGHT_IMAGE=resident-hindsight-base:1.2.1 \
+  -t resident-hindsight:1.2.1-policy1 .
+```
+
+Use self-hosted PostgreSQL with pgvector and native full text search. Apply
+migrations in one privileged startup job, then start one API process and one
+standalone worker using the same image, protected policy and common environment:
+
+```sh
+hindsight-admin run-db-migration --schema public
+hindsight-api --host 0.0.0.0 --port 8888 --workers 1
+hindsight-worker --worker-id resident-memory-1 --http-host 0.0.0.0 --http-port 8889
+```
+
+These are three separate process commands, not a shell pipeline. Set
+`HINDSIGHT_API_WORKER_ENABLED=false` **only on the API process**. Start both from
+the image's `/opt/resident-service` working directory: its empty, trusted `.env`
+stops upstream dotenv discovery from overriding host settings. Run the filesystem
+read-only, with only necessary temporary directories writable; mount policy read-only.
+The image selects the upstream non-root `hindsight` user. The runtime UID must be
+able to read policy; require dropped capabilities and no-new-privileges in the host
+deployment. These image defaults do not enforce the host's filesystem or egress
+boundary. Do not mount project directories here.
+The image enables both policy extensions by default, so a missing policy file
+fails startup instead of silently starting an unauthenticated server.
+
+`service.env.example` is a configuration template; replace every placeholder and
+inject secrets from the host. It disables MCP, the control plane (not in this
+image), bank configuration writes, dry-run extraction, file uploads, provider batch
+jobs and autonomous reconciliation/model/page sweeps. Observations remain enabled;
+the supervisor explicitly admits consolidation after budget checks. One of the two
+worker slots is reserved for consolidation, leaving one for retain; the upstream
+default reservation of two would starve retain at a total of two slots.
+
+All LLM **and embedding** requests go through one host-controlled gateway with hard
+budget admission, model allowlists, request size/time limits and no fallback to
+another cloud. The existing `bank-llm-routing` extension rejects unmatched LLM
+bank/schema/provider/model combinations. It does not gate embeddings or enforce a
+budget: gateway admission and network egress policy do that. Allow service egress
+only to PostgreSQL and this gateway, including DNS policy; provide no vendor keys,
+provider fallback members, or per-operation/embedding override environment values.
+Startup LLM verification is skipped because it has no bank and must not bypass the
+route policy. Qualify the configured gateway with a canary before admitting work.
+
+Local embeddings can be introduced as a separate approved service behind the
+same gateway contract. Changing an embedding model/dimension is a data migration
+and reindexing decision, not a silent configuration edit.
+
+## Verification and recovery
+
+```sh
+uv sync --frozen --package hindsight-api-slim --extra embedded-db
+.venv/bin/python -m pytest -q hindsight-extensions/resident/tests \
+  -c hindsight-extensions/resident/pyproject.toml
+uv run --project hindsight-system-tests pytest \
+  hindsight-system-tests/tests/test_resident_extension.py
+scripts/hooks/lint.sh
+```
+
+The blackbox story uses the published client, a real split API/worker deployment,
+embedded PostgreSQL and the upstream deterministic provider stub. It checks denied
+credentials/banks/operations/tags, inaccessible source documents, async retry
+idempotency, completed consolidation and restricted retrieval. Unit tests also
+check every bank hook, policy failure and stale queued credentials. No live provider
+credential is needed. In environments with a SOCKS proxy, install the `socksio`
+optional transport dependency in the API and system-test environments first.
+
+Probe API `/health` on port 8888 and worker `/health` on 8889, then perform a bounded
+canary retain, poll its operation to completion, recall its source and verify a
+forbidden bank request fails. A successful health response alone does not prove
+policy, gateway or memory correctness. The production readiness/admission controller
+must check the applied release/policy digest and budget gate as well.
+
+Revocation is an explicit rollout: pause admission, stop/drain API and worker,
+replace the protected policy on both, restart, and reconcile queued operations.
+Already running provider requests are not magically recalled. Revoke at the gateway
+and stop the worker for an immediate spend fence. Replaying or dropping an operation
+requires a recorded supervisor decision; do not assume a key change deletes data.
+
+Keep PostgreSQL backups/PITR plus full-bank transfer exports. Memory observations,
+mental models and pages contain durable derived knowledge and are not a disposable
+cache. Document export defaults omit observations and knowledge-base content. A
+full-bank export includes current data/config and model/page content; history is
+partly opt-in (mental-model history travels with model data), and observation
+history is omitted. A logical export is not a complete database backup. Quiesce writers
+for consistent logical exports. Roll back the image and policy together, and use a
+compatible database snapshot when migrations are not backward compatible.
